2|0@india^1439939465#cu7mhx6:Aris_Throttle$:South Korea was an exception in the sense that they went for differentiated exports right from the beginning and were never a mass-producer of commodities as Japan used to be and China now is. It would be interesting to study their development story (that I'm frankly not too familiar with). Singapore and Hong Kong are simply city-states who have taken advantage of their location as Western gateway to Asia. I don't think, there is any tangible learning a country like India can take from the growth of these two cities. Quite frankly, we need to fully exploit our strategic advantages (young population, wage differential, growing technically educated middle class etc) and simultaneously carve our own path towards development. But, unfortunately, the political system is so rotten that there is no real will to grow. Elections every six month occupy the attention of our governments (look at the shambles over GST) and there is simply no long-term vision and determination to take our country to the growth path.
